# 1. Hello world

HQL is a strongly typed expression and query language. You use it to read
data, transform it and write it out: records from a JSON file, rows of a
table, events from a stream, the notes in a folder of Markdown documents.
Where a shell pipeline would combine `jq`, `grep` and a script, an HQL program
is one expression that `hql` checks and runs.

Most of the data a query language handles was written by someone else, and
its shape is not guaranteed. HQL deals with this through types. Every value
has a type, and a type is a contract: it states what the value must look
like. `hql` checks a program against these contracts before running it, and
checks data against them when the data is read. A mistake in the program is
reported before anything runs. A mistake in the data is reported where the
data comes in, not three steps later.

A program goes through the same stages every time:

```d2
direction: down

program: "program\nfmt(\"Hello, {}!\", \"world\")" {
  shape: page
}
check: "check\ninfer and check every type" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
evaluate: "evaluate\ncompute the value" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
value: "value\n\"Hello, world!\"" {
  shape: page
}
check_error: "check error\nnothing runs" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
}
execute_failure: "execute error or failure\nthe run stops" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
}

program -> check
check -> evaluate: "the program fits its types"
evaluate -> value: "hql writes it"
check -> check_error: "a type does not fit" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
evaluate -> execute_failure: "bad data, division by zero" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
```

## Hello, world

The smallest program that says hello is the text itself:

```hql
"Hello, world!"
```

```text
"Hello, world!"
```

A program is an **expression**, and `hql` writes its value. A string prints
in quotes, the way it is written in a program. `fmt` builds a string from a
template, filling each `{}` with the next argument:

```hql
fmt("Hello, {}!", "world")
```

```text
"Hello, world!"
```

## Running a program

`hql eval` runs a program given on the command line:

```sh
$ hql eval 'fmt("Hello, {}!", "world")'
"Hello, world!"
```

`hql run` runs a program from a file. A file holds the same thing an
argument does:

```sh
$ cat hello.hql
title = "Dune"
pages = 412
fmt("{} has {} pages", title, pages)
$ hql run hello.hql
"Dune has 412 pages"
```

`hql repl` reads one program at a time and prints each value, which is
useful for trying things out. [[02-data-types|Chapter 2]] starts there.

This book shows each program in a block, followed by what `hql` writes for
it. Most programs run against a small example vault, a reading club of books
and authors in
[`examples/reading-club/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/examples/reading-club).
To follow along, clone the repository and pass the vault to `hql`:

```sh
$ hql --vault examples/reading-club eval 'cards | size'
15
```

## Values and pipelines

Every value has a type. `412` is an `Int`, `"Dune"` is a `String`, and
`[412, 280, 310]` is a `List<Int>`. Operators and functions combine values
into new ones:

```hql
412 / 100
```

```text
4
```

Both operands are `Int`, so `/` divides whole numbers and drops the
remainder.

Most HQL programs are **pipelines**. `|` passes the value on its left to the
step on its right, and each step returns a new value for the next one:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | filter(n => n > 300) | size
```

```text
2
```

`filter` keeps the elements for which a function returns `true`. Here the
function is `n => n > 300`, a **lambda**: a function written in place, with
its parameter before `=>` and its result after it. `size` counts what is
left. [[03-collections|Chapter 3]] covers the steps, and
[[04-functions|chapter 4]] covers functions.

## Bindings

`name = expression` binds a value to a name. The name can be used in the
statements after it, and the value of the program is its last expression:

```hql
pages = 280
chapters = 24
pages / chapters
```

```text
11
```

A binding can state its type. The checker then rejects a value of any other
type:

```hql
rating : Int = "five"
rating
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `rating` is declared Int but its value is String
 --> <expression>:1:16
  |
1 | rating : Int = "five"
  |                ^^^^^^
```

Without the declaration, the checker infers the type from the value. A
declaration is useful where you want the program to state its intent, and
for values whose type cannot be inferred, such as an empty list.

## Checked before it runs

`hql` checks the whole program before it evaluates any of it. A **check
error** names the problem and points at the expression:

```hql
1 + "two"
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: addition requires two Int, two Float or two Decimal operands
 --> <expression>:1:5
  |
1 | 1 + "two"
  |     ^^^^^
```

Nothing was evaluated. There is no value, only the report, and `hql` exits
with status `1`. A name that was never bound is a check error too:

```hql
pages + 1
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `pages` is not bound
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | pages + 1
  | ^^^^^
```

`hql check` runs only the checker. It prints the type of the program and
evaluates nothing:

```sh
$ hql check - <<< '412 / 100'
Int
```

## Errors while it runs

Some problems depend on values, and the checker cannot see values. Dividing
by zero passes the check and fails when the program runs:

```hql
pages = 412
pages / 0
```

```text
execute failure[division-by-zero]: division by zero
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | pages / 0
  | ^^^^^^^^^
```

The report starts with the **phase** in which the problem was found: `check`
before the program runs, `execute` while it runs. Data that does not match
its type is also found in the execute phase, when the data is read.
[[02-data-types|Chapter 2]] shows how, and [[12-errors|chapter 12]] covers
errors in full.

## Choosing a value

`a given condition otherwise b` is `a` when the condition is true, and `b`
when it is false:

```hql
pages = 412
"long" given pages > 300 otherwise "short"
```

```text
"long"
```

It is an expression, so it has a value and a type. Both branches must have
the same type.

`match` compares a value with a list of patterns in order. The first pattern
that fits gives the result:

```hql
status = "reading"
match status {
    "done" => "finished",
    "reading" => "on the nightstand",
    _ => "on the shelf",
}
```

```text
"on the nightstand"
```

`_` fits any value. A `match` must cover every possible value, and the
checker rejects one that does not:

```hql
match 3 { 1 => "one", 2 => "two" }
```

```text
check error[non-exhaustive-match]: this match does not cover every Int: add a `_` arm
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | match 3 { 1 => "one", 2 => "two" }
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

## What is next

[[02-data-types|Chapter 2]] covers types as contracts, and the types values
are built from: numbers, text, dates and times, and `Data`, the tree that
JSON and YAML documents are read into.
